"Distill Until Divergence:Error-Truncated Hindsight Distillation for Agentic RL (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23048v1)\nciteturn41224view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23048v1\",\"lineno\":182}); Total lines: 412\nL109: ## 3 Method\nL110: \nL111: ### 3.1 Overview\nL112: \nL113: The remainder of this section is organized as follows. Section cite13†3.2 formalizes the multi-turn agent setting, and Section cite14†3.3 presents the empirical observations that motivate our design. Section cite15†3.4 then introduces the ETRO algorithm.\nL114: \nL115: cite74†Image: Refer to caption Figure 2: Overview of ETRO’s trajectory-aware routing of Positive and Negative hindsight credit.\nL116: ### 3.2 Problem Formulation\nL117: Let $\\mathcal{D}$ be a collection of tasks and $\\mathcal{E}$ an interactive environment or external tool. Given a task $x\\sim\\mathcal{D}$ and an initial observation $o_{1}$, the agent interacts with $\\mathcal{E}$ to produce a trajectory $\\tau=(x,o_{1},u_{1},\\ldots,u_{T})$, where $T$ is the number of interaction turns.\nL118: Each turn $u_{t}=(y_{t},a_{t},o_{t+1})$ consists of the model-generated response $y_{t}$, the executable action $a_{t}$ extracted from that response, and the resulting environment observation $o_{t+1}$. The response contains reasoning and a task-specific action or final answer, generated by the policy $\\pi_{\\theta}$ conditioned on the task and preceding interaction history. The trajectory ends with a final answer, environment completion, or exhaustion of the turn budget.\nL119: A task-specific verifier assigns a binary training outcome indicating success or failure. For trajectory $i$, we use $\\tau_{i}$ and $T_{i}$ to denote the trajectory and its number of turns, and $y_{i,t,j}$ to denote token $j$ of the model response at turn $t$.\nL120: ### 3.3 Preliminary Study: Regional Alignment of OPD Credit\nL121: We investigate whether the alignment between skill-induced OPD signals and outcome-based action quality varies across trajectory regions. A GRPO-trained Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct actor samples eight complete trajectories per task prompt. The writer summarizes reusable workflows from successful and failed trajectories as Positive skills and failure-specific corrections as Negative skills.\nL122: On failed trajectories, we quantify each skill’s influence by averaging the difference between skill-conditioned and unconditioned log-probabilities over each turn’s reasoning and action tokens. We assess alignment through Spearman correlations with step-level advantages derived from GiGPO’s state–action grouping approach (cite75†Feng et al., 2026 ), separately before, at, and after the diagnosed error.\nL123: These advantages compare pooled action returns against a shared-state baseline; estimation and filtering details appear in Appendix cite45†C.1 .\nL124: OPD modulation shows region-dependent correlations with the action-pooled reference advantage, with stronger positive associations in the prefix for Positive skills and at the diagnosed error for Negative skills than in the post-error suffix. As shown in Figure cite76†1 , Positive modulation has a correlation of $0.59$ in the prefix but $0.01$ in the suffix; Negative modulation has a correlation of $0.46$ at the diagnosed error but $-0.05$ in the suffix.\nL125: The weak suffix correlations suggest that, after an error changes the interaction state, skill-induced probability shifts may no longer align with outcome-based action quality. This potential mismatch motivates testing Positive guidance before the error and Negative guidance at the error, while removing suffix OPD to avoid extending potentially mismatched skill supervision and retaining GRPO to learn from the trajectory’s actual outcome.\nL126: ### 3.4 Error-Truncated Polarity-Routed Distillation\nL127: Figure cite77†2 illustrates how ETRO routes hindsight supervision using a writer-estimated error boundary. For each completed trajectory $\\tau_{i}$, the writer produces a Positive skill $z_{i}^{+}$ that summarizes reusable task-solving strategies to reinforce effective decisions through OPD.\nL128: For a failed trajectory, it also identifies the earliest turn $k_{i}$ whose erroneous decision is judged decisive for the observed failure and remains uncorrected by later actions, and produces a Negative skill $z_{i}^{-}$ describing the decision, its consequence, and an executable alternative. This boundary separates Positive-guided decisions before the error from the local Negative correction at the error and the GRPO-only suffix.\nL129: Failed trajectories without a valid boundary retain Positive guidance throughout; we represent this case by $k_{i}=\\infty$. Writer prompts, boundary validation, and skill construction details appear in Appendix cite27†A .\nL130: Let $c_{i,t,j}$ denote the original generation context of token $y_{i,t,j}$, comprising the task, preceding interaction history, and earlier tokens in the current response. Let $p\\in\\{+,-\\}$ index the skill polarity, with $z_{i}^{p}$ denoting the corresponding skill. We compute the OPD gap:\nL131: \nL132:  | $\\displaystyle\\Delta^{p}_{i,t,j}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\log\\pi_{\\mathrm{old}}(y_{i,t,j}\\mid c_{i,t,j},z_{i}^{p})$  |  | (1)\nL133:  |  | $\\displaystyle-\\log\\pi_{\\mathrm{old}}(y_{i,t,j}\\mid c_{i,t,j}),$  |\nL134: Here, $\\pi_{\\mathrm{old}}$ is the frozen rollout policy, and $\\Delta^{p}_{i,t,j}$ is fixed during optimization. Skills provide additional context only for re-scoring and are absent during rollout and evaluation.\nL135: \nL136: The unweighted routed advantage for each eligible reasoning or action token is\nL137:  | $$A^{\\mathrm{OPD}}_{i,t,j}=\\begin{cases}\\Delta^{+}_{i,t,j},&\\text{if $\\tau_{i}$ succeeds},\\\\\nL138: \\Delta^{+}_{i,t,j},&\\text{if $\\tau_{i}$ fails and $t<k_{i}$},\\\\\nL139: \\Delta^{-}_{i,t,j},&\\text{if $\\tau_{i}$ fails and $t=k_{i}$},\\\\\nL140: 0,&\\text{otherwise}.\\end{cases}$$  |  | (2)\nL141: \nL142: We weight this auxiliary advantage by $\\lambda$ and add it to the trajectory-level GRPO advantage:\nL143: \nL144:  | $$A^{\\mathrm{ETRO}}_{i,t,j}=A_{i}^{\\mathrm{GRPO}}+\\lambda A^{\\mathrm{OPD}}_{i,t,j}.$$  |  | (3)\nL145: Here, $A_{i}^{\\mathrm{GRPO}}$ is the group-relative outcome advantage of trajectory $i$, and $\\lambda$ controls the strength of both OPD branches. We substitute $A^{\\mathrm{ETRO}}_{i,t,j}$ into the standard clipped GRPO objective, setting the OPD term to zero outside eligible reasoning and action spans. Post-error truncation therefore removes only auxiliary supervision; GRPO continues to optimize every valid response token in the complete rollout.\nL146: ## 4 Experiments\nL147: Table 1: Results on ALFWorld, Search QA, and WebShop across Qwen2.5-Instruct model scales (%). Higher values indicate better performance.\nL148: Method  | ALFWorld  | Search QA  | WebShop\nL149:  | Succ.  | NQ  | Triv  | Pop  | Hotp  | 2Wk  | MuS  | Bam  | Avg.  | Score  | Succ.\nL150: Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct\nL151: ---\nL152: GRPO  | 65.7  | 16.6  | 34.1  | 15.9  | 16.1  | 24.8  | 3.4  | 58.1  | 24.1  | 72.0  | 62.0\nL153: GRPO+OPSD  | 71.1  | 16.9  | 34.3  | 16.4  | 16.3  | 24.8  | 3.4  | 56.9  | 24.1  | 84.9  | 73.4\nL154: Skill-SD  | 68.0  | 16.7  | 35.4  | 14.8  | 16.7  | 25.2  | 3.9  | 11.2  | 17.7  | 80.6  | 68.8\nL155: RLSD  | 67.2  | 13.9  | 29.4  | 12.4  | 14.2  | 21.1  | 3.0  | 9.5  | 14.8  | 80.5  | 63.3\nL156: SDAR  | 72.7  | 16.1  | 32.7  | 15.8  | 15.9  | 24.4  | 3.3  | 4.4  | 16.1  | 81.9  | 64.1\nL157: ICSD  | 73.4  | 18.3  | 39.2  | 17.1  | 18.7  | 28.2  | 4.4  | 68.0  | 27.7  | 85.6  | 75.8\nL158: ETRO  | 79.1  | 19.4  | 40.8  | 18.7  | 20.1  | 30.4  | 5.2  | 69.8  | 29.2  | 87.9  | 80.8\nL159: Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct\nL160: ---\nL161: GRPO  | 75.0  | 39.3  | 60.6  | 41.1  | 37.4  | 34.6  | 15.4  | 26.4  | 36.4  | 79.8  | 64.4\nL162: GRPO+OPSD  | 81.2  | 44.9  | 61.2  | 45.2  | 40.4  | 38.5  | 16.0  | 66.1  | 44.6  | 77.8  | 66.4\nL163: Skill-SD  | 73.4  | 44.4  | 60.4  | 44.0  | 39.5  | 40.4  | 15.4  | 64.9  | 44.1  | 75.9  | 64.0\nL164: RLSD  | 79.7  | 41.5  | 58.6  | 42.3  | 40.4  | 40.2  | 16.8  | 66.9  | 43.8  | 84.4  | 66.4\nL165: SDAR  | 84.4  | 44.8  | 58.1  | 44.3  | 38.6  | 36.2  | 15.7  | 66.1  | 43.4  | 85.0  | 68.0\nL166: OPID  | 84.3  | 45.9  | 61.4  | 45.7  | 40.7  | 38.8  | 16.4  | 66.1  | 45.0  | 85.0  | 74.2\nL167: SEED  | 91.8  | 44.3  | 61.9  | 47.2  | 41.0  | 42.0  | 16.9  | 67.7  | 45.9  | 88.5  | 78.9\nL168: ETRO  | 93.3  | 47.2  | 63.8  | 48.5  | 43.2  | 44.1  | 18.7  | 70.8  | 48.0  | 90.3  | 83.0\nL169: Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct\nL170: ---\nL171: GRPO  | 81.2  | 45.1  | 63.7  | 44.0  | 43.6  | 43.2  | 16.8  | 37.6  | 42.0  | 80.9  | 73.7\nL172: GRPO+OPSD  | 80.4  | 47.3  | 64.5  | 46.9  | 43.8  | 39.3  | 18.0  | 69.4  | 47.0  | 86.8  | 76.5\nL173: Skill-SD  | 85.1  | 47.1  | 64.5  | 47.8  | 44.2  | 42.1  | 20.2  | 69.0  | 47.8  | 86.1  | 76.5\nL174: RLSD  | 82.0  | 46.8  | 63.0  | 44.4  | 45.5  | 48.9  | 21.5  | 73.0  | 49.0  | 87.4  | 77.3\nL175: SDAR  | 85.9  | 46.3  | 63.5  | 48.2  | 43.8  | 48.4  | 19.6  | 73.0  | 49.0  | 89.4  | 82.8\nL176: OPID  | 90.0  | 48.8  | 65.6  | 46.8  | 46.1  | 42.7  | 21.7  | 72.6  | 49.2  | 85.3  | 79.7\nL177: SEED  | 96.1  | 47.0  | 64.9  | 47.8  | 45.2  | 45.3  | 20.1  | 70.2  | 48.6  | 89.7  | 78.1\nL178: ETRO  | 97.0  | 50.4  | 67.1  | 49.5  | 48.0  | 50.2  | 23.1  | 74.0  | 51.8  | 91.7  | 85.8\nL179: ### 4.1 Experimental Setup\nL180: \nL181: #### Benchmarks and metrics.\nL182: \nL183: We evaluate ETRO on WebShop (cite78†Yao et al., 2022 ), ALFWorld (cite52†Shridhar et al., 2020 ), and Search QA. We report normalized score and exact success for WebShop, success rate for ALFWorld, and average answer accuracy across the seven Search QA datasets.\nL184: #### Models and baselines.\nL185: We train Qwen2.5-Instruct models at 1.5B, 3B, and 7B scales and compare against GRPO, GRPO+OPSD, Skill-SD, RLSD, SDAR, OPID (cite55†Yang et al., 2026b ), SEED (cite56†Wu et al., 2026 ), and ICSD (cite69†Lan et al., 2026 ). In Table cite79†1 , ETRO results come from our experiments, whereas all baseline results are taken from prior reports rather than locally reproduced under a unified protocol. Skills are absent during ETRO evaluation.\nL186: Result provenance and our training and evaluation settings are described in Appendix cite30†B .\nL187: #### Training configuration.\nL188: \nL189: We sample eight rollouts per task and optimize with AdamW at learning rate $10^{-6}$, using one PPO epoch per batch and clipping parameter 0.2. A frozen Qwen3.5-27B writer supplies skills, with OPD coefficient $\\lambda=0.001$.\nL190: #### Evaluation protocol.\nL191: \nL192: WebShop evaluation covers 500 tasks and ALFWorld uses 134 Unseen tasks, both with a 15-turn limit. Search QA follows the Search-R1 splits and reports normalized exact-match accuracy averaged equally across seven datasets.\nL193: Table 2: Skill selection and branch combination on WebShop and ALFWorld using Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct.\nL194: Method  | WebShop  | ALFWorld\nL195: --- | --- | ---\nL196: Succ.  | Score  | Succ.\nL197: --- | --- | ---\nL198: GRPO  | 62.0  | 72.0  | 65.7\nL199: Global Positive  | 77.2  | 85.3  | 76.9\nL200: Global Negative  | 68.8  | 75.3  | 72.4\nL201: Global Both  | 76.0  | 83.8  | 76.9\nL202: Positive Only  | 77.6  | 85.0  | 77.6\nL203: Negative Only  | 73.4  | 79.8  | 74.6\nL204: ETRO  | 80.8  | 87.9  | 79.1\nL205: Table 3: Suffix supervision and boundary selection on WebShop and ALFWorld using Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct.\nL206: Method  | WebShop  | ALFWorld\nL207: --- | --- | ---\nL208: Succ.  | Score  | Succ.\nL209: --- | --- | ---\nL210: GRPO  | 62.0  | 72.0  | 65.7\nL211: Suffix Positive  | 77.6  | 84.6  | 77.6\nL212: Suffix Negative  | 72.2  | 79.3  | 74.6\nL213: Random Boundary  | 72.8  | 79.5  | 73.1\nL214: ETRO  | 80.8  | 87.9  | 79.1\nL215: ### 4.2 Main Results\nL216: \nL217: Compared with GRPO and its self-distillation extension GRPO+OPSD, ETRO improves performance across all three domains and model scales in Table cite79†1 . At 1.5B, its WebShop success reaches 80.8%, exceeding GRPO by 18.8 percentage points. At 3B, its Search QA macro-average accuracy reaches 48.0%, surpassing GRPO+OPSD by 3.4 points.\nL218: Compared with related distillation methods, ETRO also achieves the highest reported results among the listed methods at each scale. At 1.5B, its WebShop and ALFWorld success rates exceed ICSD by 5.0 and 5.7 points; at 3B, they exceed SEED by 4.1 and 1.5 points, respectively. At 7B, ETRO improves over SDAR’s WebShop success by 3.0 points and OPID’s Search QA macro-average accuracy by 2.6 points.\nL219: These gains extend beyond outcome-only training to existing distillation approaches across interactive task execution and search-augmented reasoning.\nL220: ### 4.3 Ablation Studies\nL221: Skill Selection and Branch Combination. To assess skill selection and branch combination, we organize the Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct comparisons on WebShop and ALFWorld into five groups (Table cite80†3 ): GRPO without OPD; Global Positive and Global Negative with one skill applied throughout the trajectory; Global Both with both skills combined globally; Positive Only and Negative Only with one branch removed from ETRO’s routing rule; and the complete ETRO method.\nL222: Positive Only retains ETRO’s Positive guidance on successful trajectories, failed-trajectory prefixes, and complete failed trajectories without a valid boundary, while Negative Only retains its Negative guidance at diagnosed errors. All OPD variants use the same coefficient $\\lambda=0.001$ and retain GRPO over the complete rollout; branch removal only changes the coverage of auxiliary supervision.\nL223: The complete method outperforms all variants on the three reported metrics, reaching 80.8% WebShop success and 79.1% ALFWorld success. On WebShop, it exceeds Global Positive and Global Negative by 3.6 and 12.0 percentage points. Removing the Negative or Positive branch lowers WebShop success to 77.6% or 73.4% and ALFWorld success to 77.6% or 74.6%, respectively, supporting the complementary roles of workflow guidance and local correction.\nL224: Global Both averages the two skill-induced gaps throughout failed trajectories while retaining Positive guidance on successful trajectories. Its WebShop success of 76.0% remains below ETRO, suggesting that combining skills globally is less effective than routing them by trajectory region.\nL225: Suffix Supervision and Boundary Selection. To assess where supervision should end once both branches are available, we compare ETRO with Suffix Positive, Suffix Negative, and Random Boundary using Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct (Table cite80†3 ). The two suffix variants keep ETRO’s Positive prefix and Negative error-turn guidance but continue the corresponding skill after the diagnosed error.\nL226: Random Boundary keeps the same Positive–Negative–Off routing structure but partitions each eligible failed trajectory at a randomly selected turn instead of the diagnosed error. All variants retain GRPO over the complete rollout and the same OPD coefficient.\nL227: Truncation at the diagnosed error outperforms continued suffix supervision and random-boundary routing on all reported metrics (Table cite80†3 ). On WebShop, ETRO exceeds Suffix Positive and Suffix Negative by 3.2 and 8.6 percentage points in success. Random Boundary reaches 72.8% success, falling below ETRO by 8.0 points despite also removing suffix OPD.\nL228: The consistent improvements in WebShop score and ALFWorld success further favor truncation at the diagnosed boundary over extending supervision or selecting an arbitrary cutoff. Section cite24†4.4 separately examines the placement of local Negative correction.\nL229: Figure 3: Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct training dynamics on WebShop for pure GRPO, full-trajectory Positive and Negative OPD, and ETRO. Training reward is reported in $[0,1]$. Validation uses a 15-turn interaction limit.\nL230: Strength of Suffix Supervision. To distinguish a benefit from weakening suffix supervision from a benefit of removing it, we retain diagnosis-based boundary selection and vary the post-error OPD coefficient as $\\alpha\\lambda$, with $\\alpha\\in\\{0,0.25,0.5,1\\}$ and $\\lambda=0.001$, using Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct on WebShop (Table cite81†4 ). We evaluate both Positive and Negative suffix supervision. Prefix and error-turn routing, successful-trajectory supervision, and the no-boundary fallback remain unchanged.\nL231: All arms use the same initialization, training settings, writer, boundary-selection procedure, evaluation protocol, and loss normalization. The shared $\\alpha=0$ endpoint is ETRO; $\\alpha=1$ corresponds to full-strength Suffix Positive or Suffix Negative.\nL232: Complete truncation achieves the highest success rate and score in both sweeps (Table cite81†4 ). Reducing $\\alpha$ from 1 to 0.25 raises WebShop success from 77.6% to 79.0% for Positive suffix guidance and from 72.2% to 76.6% for Negative guidance. Both trail ETRO’s 80.8% by 1.8 and 4.2 points. Both variants also remain below ETRO in task score. Thus, weakening suffix supervision improves both variants relative to full-strength supervision, but none of the tested nonzero weights matches complete removal.\nL233: Table 4: Post-error OPD weight sweep on WebShop using Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct, with $\\lambda=0.001$.\nL234: Method  | $\\alpha$  | Success (%)  | Score\nL235: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL236: ETRO  | 0  | 80.8  | 87.9\nL237: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL238: Suffix Positive  | 0.25  | 79.0  | 85.1\nL239: Suffix Positive  | 0.5  | 78.4  | 85.2\nL240: Suffix Positive  | 1  | 77.6  | 84.6\nL241: Suffix Negative  | 0.25  | 76.6  | 82.7\nL242: Suffix Negative  | 0.5  | 74.4  | 80.6\nL243: Suffix Negative  | 1  | 72.2  | 79.3\nL244: ### 4.4 Analysis Experiments\nL245: \nL246: Training behavior. To examine whether ETRO’s final gains are accompanied by sustained improvement during optimization, we compare GRPO, Global Positive, Global Negative, and ETRO using Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct on WebShop. We track unsmoothed training reward, validation success, and policy entropy at the same recorded checkpoints (Figure cite82†3 ), assessing task performance alongside changes in policy concentration.\nL247: ETRO maintains the highest validation success at every recorded checkpoint without a monotonic decrease in policy entropy. Its success rises from 47.8% at step 20 to 80.8% at step 160, exceeding the final Global Positive, Global Negative, and GRPO results by 3.6, 12.0, and 18.8 percentage points, respectively. Training reward reaches 0.9531 at step 120 and fluctuates afterward, while validation success continues to improve.\nL248: Over the same late-stage interval, ETRO’s entropy recovers from 0.351 to 0.804, whereas Global Negative finishes at 0.363 with lower success. These observations show that the reported performance gains coexist with sustained validation improvement and a recovery in response diversity, rather than uniformly increasing policy concentration.\nL249: Negative-skill placement. To examine whether failure-specific guidance benefits from placement at the decision it describes, we vary the location of the one-turn Negative OPD mask using Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct on WebShop. For failed trajectories with nonempty prefixes and suffixes, the writer produces a Negative skill for the validated error at turn $k$.\nL250: Holding that skill’s content fixed, we apply the mask either at $k$, at a randomly sampled turn from the pre-error prefix, or at a randomly sampled turn from the post-error suffix. Only the location of Negative supervision varies; the supervised turn count remains fixed.\nL251: Applying Negative guidance at the diagnosed error achieves the highest success and score among the three tested placements (Figure cite83†4 ). Error-turn conditioning reaches 80.8% success and an 87.9 score, compared with 75.4% and 84.3 for prefix placement and 71.8% and 81.5 for suffix placement. The corresponding success gains are 5.4 and 9.0 percentage points, with score gains of 3.6 and 6.4.\nL252: These results favor localizing the correction signal at the diagnosed decision rather than shifting it to an earlier or later turn.\nL253: Writer reliability. To assess the quality of the skills and error boundaries that support routing, we evaluate 100 failed WebShop trajectories sampled after Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct training. Five annotators each inspect 20 distinct complete trajectories to establish reference annotations. Using these human annotations as reference answers, GPT-5.6 sol evaluates the writer outputs for boundary acceptance, exact-turn agreement, workflow validity, and correction validity. Results appear in Table cite84†5 .\nL254: The reference-based evaluation supports the quality of the writer-generated skills and error boundaries on the sampled WebShop trajectories. Boundary acceptance reaches 92.0%, with 84.0% exact-turn agreement against human references; workflow and correction validity reach 92.0% and 90.0%, respectively.\nL255: These results support the writer’s ability to identify relevant error boundaries and produce actionable guidance for both OPD branches, providing empirical support for the skill generation and localization underlying ETRO’s routing.\nL256: Table 5: Writer quality on WebShop, evaluated against human references by GPT-5.6 sol (%).\nL257: Metric  | Rate (%)\nL258: --- | ---\nL259: Boundary acceptance  | 92.0\nL260: Exact-turn agreement  | 84.0\nL261: Workflow validity  | 92.0\nL262: Correction validity  | 90.0\nL263: Figure 4: Comparison of Negative-skill conditioning regions on WebShop using Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct.\nL264: Post-error recovery. To assess whether suffix truncation improves behavior after a recoverable error, we compare actors from the same perturbed states on held-out WebShop tasks, collecting repeated continuations without hindsight skills. The actors retain Positive prefix and Negative error-turn guidance during training but differ in suffix treatment: Suffix Positive and Suffix Negative continue the corresponding OPD signal, while ETRO retains only GRPO.\nL265: We report recovery success, normalized score, wrong-branch continuation, and first-turn action entropy; the controlled-error construction and evaluation procedure appear in Appendix cite49†C.2 .\nL266: ETRO achieves the highest recovery success and score and the lowest wrong-branch continuation rate among the compared actors (Table cite85†6 ). It reaches 63.8% success and an 82.6 score, compared with 54.6% and 74.8 for Suffix Positive and 49.2% and 69.7 for Suffix Negative. Wrong-branch continuation falls to 23.7%, versus 33.9% and 40.6%, reductions of 10.2 and 16.9 percentage points. ETRO’s first-turn entropy is 0.62, compared with 0.55 and 0.39 for the suffix variants.\nL267: These results indicate that removing post-error skill supervision while retaining GRPO helps agents recover from errors rather than persist along an incorrect action branch. Without hindsight skills at evaluation, these differences reflect learned behavior. Higher entropy accompanies better recovery and fewer wrong-branch continuations, rather than action variability alone.\nL268: Table 6: Post-error recovery on WebShop.\nL269: Method  | Recovery  | Recovery  | Wrong  | Entropy\nL270: --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL271: Success  | Score  | Branch\nL272: --- | --- | ---\nL273: Suffix Positive  | 54.6  | 74.8  | 33.9  | 0.55\nL274: Suffix Negative  | 49.2  | 69.7  | 40.6  | 0.39\nL275: ETRO  | 63.8  | 82.6  | 23.7  | 0.62\nL276: ## 5 Conclusion\nL277: We presented ETRO to address the mismatch between hindsight supervision and evolving interaction states. By separating workflow guidance from local error correction, ETRO routes OPD by skill polarity and trajectory position while preserving GRPO throughout the rollout. Experiments on WebShop, ALFWorld, and Search QA demonstrate competitive performance across model scales.\nL278: Ablations support complementary skill routing and post-error truncation, while recovery experiments show improved recovery and less wrong-branch continuation. These findings suggest that effective hindsight credit allocation depends on both skill content and its temporal applicability. Future work could extend routing to multiple error and recovery boundaries.\nL279: ## Limitations\nL280: \nL281: ETRO depends on the writer to locate errors and generate hindsight skills. Our audit and ablation studies support the effectiveness of this design, but its performance may vary with the quality of the writer’s outputs.\nL282: \nL283: Finally, hindsight generation and policy re-scoring add training-time computation even though skills are absent at inference time. The approach depends on access to complete trajectories and outcome feedback, which may be costly or unavailable in some applications.\nL284: ## Ethical Considerations\nL285: \nL286: Training language-model agents to act more effectively can also increase the consequences of unsafe actions. Success in benchmark environments does not establish safety for deployment with real purchases, private information, or unrestricted external tools. Such deployment would require additional safeguards, authorization checks, and task-specific evaluation beyond the scope of this work.\nL287: Interaction trajectories and hindsight skills can contain user requests, retrieved text, or other sensitive information. Any release or reuse of these artifacts should respect applicable dataset and model licenses, protect personal information, and preserve the anonymity of review materials. Writer-generated diagnoses are fallible and should not be treated as authoritative judgments of users or annotators.\nL288: ## AI Assistance Disclosure\nL289: \nL290: An AI assistant helped edit manuscript wording and formatting, including initial drafts of the Limitations and Ethical Considerations sections. The authors are responsible for verifying the final text, references, and reported results.\nL291: ## References\nL292:   * Ahmadian et al. (2024) A. Ahmadian, C. Cremer, M. Gallé, M. Fadaee, J. Kreutzer, O. Pietquin, A. Üstün, and S. Hooker Back to basics: revisiting reinforce-style optimization for learning from human feedback in llms. In Proceedings of the 62nd Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (Volume 1: Long Papers), pp. 12248–12267. Cited by: cite86†§2 .\nL293:   * Feng et al. (2026) L. Feng, Z. Xue, T. Liu, and B. An Group-in-group policy optimization for llm agent training. Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems 38, pp. 46375–46408. Cited by: cite87†§3.3 .\nL294:   * Huang et al. (2026) Y. Huang, B. Xu, H. Cao, and C. Zhu BiCAA: bidirectional credit assignment for search-augmented agent. arXiv preprint arXiv:2608.01321. Cited by: cite88†§1 .\nL295:   * Hübotter et al. (2026) J. Hübotter, F. Lübeck, L. Behric, A. Baumann, M. Bagatella, D. Marta, I. Hakimi, I. Shenfeld, T. K. Buening, C. Guestrin, et al. Reinforcement learning via self-distillation. arXiv preprint arXiv:2601.20802. Cited by: cite89†§2 .\nL296:   * Jin et al. (2025) B. Jin, H. Zeng, Z. Yue, J. Yoon, S. Arik, D. Wang, H. Zamani, and J. Han Search-r1: training llms to reason and leverage search engines with reinforcement learning. arXiv preprint arXiv:2503.09516. Cited by: cite90†§B.1 , cite86†§2 .\n"
